To solve this problem, we need to calculate the average annual growth rate from 2009 to 2015 and the average annual decline rate from 2016 to 2020.

**Average Annual Growth Rate (2009-2015)**

Let's calculate the total growth from 2009 to 2015:
\begin{align*}
\text{Total Growth} &= \text{Assets in 2015 - Assets in 2009}\\
&= 36.94 - 16.54\\
&= 20.40
\end{align*}

Since this growth occurred over 6 years, we can calculate the average annual growth rate using the formula:
\begin{align*}
\text{Average Annual Growth Rate} &= \left(\dfrac{\text{Total Growth}}{\text{Assets in 2009}}\right)^{\frac{1}{\text{Number of Years}}} - 1\\
&= \left(\dfrac{20.40}{16.54}\right)^{\frac{1}{6}} - 1\\
&\approx 0.0931
\end{align*}

To express this as a percentage, multiply by 100:
\begin{align*}
\text{Average Annual Growth Rate} &\approx 0.0931 \times 100\\
&\approx \boxed{9.31\%}
\end{align*}

**Average Annual Decline Rate (2016-2020)**

Let's calculate the total decline from 2016 to 2020:
\begin{align*}
\text{Total Decline} &= \text{Assets in 2016 - Assets in 2020}\\
&= 27.00 - 20.68\\
&= 6.32
\end{align*}

Since this decline occurred over 4 years, we can calculate the average annual decline rate using the formula:
\begin{align*}
\text{Average Annual Decline Rate} &= \left(\dfrac{\text{Total Decline}}{\text{Assets in 2016}}\right)^{\frac{1}{\text{Number of Years}}} - 1\\
&= \left(\dfrac{6.32}{27.00}\right)^{\frac{1}{4}} - 1\\
&\approx -0.0439
\end{align*}

To express this as a percentage, multiply by 100:
\begin{align*}
\text{Average Annual Decline Rate} &\approx -0.0439 \times 100\\
&\approx \boxed{-4.39\%}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the average annual growth rate from 2009 to 2015 was approximately 9.31%, and the average annual decline rate from 2016 to 2020 was approximately -4.39%.

